data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

The **sync-over-async opt-in for the EF Core Cosmos DB provider is removed**. In .NET 10 you could call:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(...).AllowSynchronousIO();
```

In .NET 11, that escape hatch is gone. Every synchronous call — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()` — now throws an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

## The Fix: Go Fully Async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return `Task<T>` / `Task`, mark methods `async`
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`

The async variants come from `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` (ensure `using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;` is present). You'll also need to propagate `async/await` up through your callers (controllers, services, etc.).
